<div class="start-card"><p><strong>You will learn:</strong> how to verify an asynchronous system without guessing model wording, locate its separate failure queues and remove resources deliberately.</p><p><strong>Starting requirements:</strong> all core components are deployed, KEDA is Ready, the demo delay is disabled and the sample manuals are indexed.</p><p><strong>Shell and directory:</strong> Bash, repository root; activate the verification virtual environment in this terminal.</p></div>
<h2 id="smoke">Run the real-deployment acceptance check</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:smoke}}
<p>{{file:tools/smoke.py}} first checks ARM deployment states, the Ready Products rollout, Function discovery, event subscription and queue configuration. It verifies database readiness through Products, uploads a new Aurora manual, waits up to seven minutes for indexing, submits a temperature question with a product filter, and waits up to eight minutes for its persisted reply.</p>
<p>It checks the request/correlation IDs, a non-empty answer and a returned passage containing the known temperature specification with the correct product/manual IDs. It does <strong>not</strong> assert exact LLM wording. Its non-secret result report is saved under <code>.state/smoke-result.json</code>. Passwords stay in process memory, TLS verification uses the exact local certificate, and no credential is placed in a URL.</p>
<div class="verification"><strong>Verification:</strong> expect the tool to report a real upload → Function indexing → queue/worker → correlated answer with relevant references. That line counts as a successful deployment check only if the tool actually ran successfully in your subscription. Consult the <a href="./validation.html">validation report</a> for the maintainer's observed results.</div>
<h2 id="diagnose">Diagnose the failing boundary</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:diagnose}}
<table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Inspect</th><th>Concrete action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Upload failed</td><td>Products logs and its waiting/failed row</td><td>Check its Blob Data Contributor role and storage endpoint. Retry as a new product after the cause is fixed.</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting manual</td><td>Event subscription filter/delivery and Function discovery</td><td>Verify the private manuals container/path, Blob-extension webhook key and code publication. A pre-subscription upload needs a new product or a deliberate replay.</td></tr><tr><td>Failed indexing</td><td>Function telemetry, host poison queue, PostgreSQL connectivity</td><td>Use the manual ID in logs; fix model quota/identity/dimensions, then replay the immutable blob. Never change a ready manual in place.</td></tr><tr><td>Queued question</td><td>Outbox warnings, request queue, ScaledObject conditions</td><td>Check sender/scaler/receiver roles separately. Inspect workload federation and queue namespace.</td></tr><tr><td>Processing question</td><td>Worker logs, model throttling, message renewal and request deadline</td><td>Use the request/correlation IDs. Inspect failed/dead-lettered messages; repair the cause before resubmitting a new question.</td></tr><tr><td>Bad answer/source</td><td>Selected product, actual chunks and prompt</td><td>Read evidence, try a focused product filter and inspect retrieval count. An LLM's plausible sentence is not proof of grounding.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Function runtime traces are in the workshop's Application Insights resource. The supplied {{file:infra/function-logs.kql}} selects recent manual-ID logs and warnings. It is a small log-inspection example, not a claim that end-to-end OpenTelemetry tracing has been implemented across Java, the Function and the worker.</p>
<p>There are three distinct dead-letter/poison locations: Event Grid's private <code>event-deadletters</code> blob container, the Blob extension's <code>webjobs-blobtrigger-poison</code> host-storage queue, and each Service Bus queue's <code>$DeadLetterQueue</code> subqueue. A message arriving in one does not imply that the next component ever ran. Avoid dumping event-subscription destination URLs: the Blob webhook URL contains its system key.</p>
<h2 id="cleanup">Deliberately clean up</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:cleanup}}
<p>The cleanup tool checks the exact subscription and workshop group tag, lists the resources it will delete, names AKS's managed node resource group, and requires you to type <code>DELETE</code> plus the complete group name. It only requests deletion of the tagged workshop group; AKS removes its managed node group as part of its lifecycle. It never deletes arbitrary similarly named groups.</p>
<p>Deletion is asynchronous. Run the exact <code>az group exists</code> command printed by the tool until it returns <code>false</code>, and verify the managed node group is gone. Azure locks or policy can prevent cleanup; inspect the failed operation instead of assuming costs have stopped.</p>
<p>Key Vault secrets/vault remain soft-deleted for seven days; App Configuration uses a one-day soft-delete period. No automatic purge is performed. Names may not be immediately reusable. This workshop does not create resources in an existing shared group. Resource-provider registration remains at subscription level and is intentionally not unregistered.</p>
<p>After Azure confirms deletion, remove the local <code>workshop/.state</code> directory if you no longer need its non-secret notes, kubeconfig and short-lived TLS private key. Review the exact directory before any recursive deletion. Keep the repository source and documentation; the website itself can still run with no Azure access.</p>
<h2 id="recap">What you have exercised</h2>
<ul><li>Built, tagged, run and pushed a Java container, then inspected its AKS Deployment, Pod, Service, probes and rollout.</li><li>Used Flyway and PostgreSQL/pgvector for products, chunks, persistent work and explicit vector retrieval.</li><li>Separated Blob notifications/Event Grid/Functions from queued application communication through Service Bus.</li><li>Configured workload/managed identity, real Key Vault secrets and ordinary App Configuration settings with explicit restart behavior.</li><li>Observed KEDA scale only the worker from zero and back, with a finite producer and finite token spend.</li></ul>
<details class="optional"><summary>Optional extensions after the first successful walkthrough</summary><p>Add trusted TLS and Entra sign-in, separate migration/runtime database roles, private endpoints for public Azure services, retries with backoff/jitter and richer operational dashboards, manual-version lifecycle, tested token-aware chunking, or measured ANN indexing. Keep each extension separate from the working core baseline.</p></details>
<h3>Before you finish</h3><p>If smoke failed, retain the printed IDs and use the <a href="./maintainer.html">first-run checklist</a> to record the failing boundary. If cleanup is still running or blocked, keep monitoring costs. Marking a documentation chapter complete does not perform cleanup or validate Azure.</p>
